 { Thanks to his active queen and more compact pawn structure, Black is better. } 1... { -KEY- } Qd5 $1 { Centralizing the queen and ruling out any tricks with d4-d5. } 2. Ke3 e5 ( { a) This thrust helps Black to approach with his king; exploiting the fact that } 2... e5 3. dxe5 $2 Qxe5 { would leave the b5-pawn doomed. } ) 
 ( { b) An alternative strategy was } 2... h4 $5 { followed by ...g6-g5, in order to gain space and fix a favourable kingside structure. } ) 
3. f4 ( { Perhaps } 3. Qb4 $5 { was a better choice, preparing to swap the central pawns on the next move. } ) 
exf4+ ( { Of course not } 3... exd4 $2 4. Qxd4 Qxd4 5. Kxd4 { when Black is forced to secure a draw by } Ke6 ) 
4. Kxf4 Ke6 { The king gets ready to replace the queen on d5 at some moment. } 5. h4 f6 6. Ke3 ( { The attempt to swap queens by } 6. Qe2 Kd6 7. Qe4 { runs into } g5 8. hxg5 Qxg5 $1 9. Kf3 Qxb5 10. Qf4 Kd7 $1 { keeping the extra pawn, since } 11. Qxf6 $2 { fails to } Qf1 ) 
Qc4 7. g3 ( 7. Qb1 $5 Qc3 8. Ke2 { was more active; e.g. } f5 9. Qa2 Kd6 10. Qa8 $1 { with some counterplay. } ) 
g5 $1 { Opening a new front on the kingside. } ( 7... Kd5 $6 8. Qg2 { was less constructive. } ) 
8. hxg5 fxg5 9. Qh2 $2 { A misguided counterattack. } ( { The immediate } 9. Ke4 { was correct; e.g. } g4 10. Kf4 Kf6 11. Ke4 Qe6 12. Kd3 Qd5 13. Qf2 Kg6 { when Black keeps a clear, but not decisive advantage. } ) 
Qb3+ 10. Ke4 g4 $1 { By creating a mating threat on f3, Black finally wins a pawn, and the rest is easy for Capablanca. } 11. Qe2 ( 11. Qf2 $1 { giving up the other pawn instead was a lesser evil, e.g., } Qxb5 12. Qa2 Kf6 13. Qf2 { pushing away Black's king from the center, in order to go d4-d5 next. } ) 
Qxg3 12. Qc4+ Ke7 13. Qc8 Qf3+ 14. Ke5 Qf6+ $1 15. Kd5 Qd6+ ( { In view of } 16... Qe6 { , White resigned. } ) 

 0-1

