 { The position looks about equal, but White has a small trump in his outside passed pawn. } 1. { -KEY- } Qd4+ $1 { Bringing the queen a bit closer to the action. } ( { Less to the point was } 1. a4 $6 Qc4 { with ...d5-d4 next. } ) 
Ke6 $6 ( { Black should have challenged the opponent's idea by } 1... Qe5 { , as after } 2. Qxe5 Kxe5 { , the outside passed pawn is compensated by the active black king; e.g. } 3. a4 Kd4 4. Kf2 ( { or } 3... Ke6 3. a5 $2 Kc5 5. Kf2 Kb5 6. Ke3 Kxa5 7. Kd4 g5 $1 { and so on; } ) 
Kc4 5. f4 Kb4 6. Ke3 Kc3 $1 7. Ke2 Kc2 { with a draw by repetition. } ) 
2. Qd3 $1 Kf6 ( { This defence of g6 costs a pawn; but Gligoric wasn't keen on } 2... Qg7 3. a4 { when the a-pawn spells big trouble for Black. } ) 
3. Qxd5 Qc2+ 4. Kh3 Qc1 $6 { Exposing the queen to an exchange on f4, a fact that Taimanov exploits. } ( { a) Instead, } 4... Qc8 $2 { failed to } 5. Kh4 $1 ) 
 ( { b) But } 4... g5 $1 { would have put up more resistance - Shipov. } ) 
 ( { c) While } 4... Qf2 5. f4 Qf1 6. Qg2 Qd1 { also offered him drawing chances - Taimanov. } ) 
5. Qd4+ $1 Ke6 ( { a) The king is forced to abandon the pawns, since both } 5... Kf7 6. Qf4 ) 
 ( { b) And } 5... Kg5 6. Qe5 Kh6 7. Qf4 $1 { let White swap queens. Similarly, the king can't go to the f-file next move either. } ) 
6. Qe4+ Kd7 7. Kh4 $1 { Very nice, parrying the mate and activating the king at the same time. } Qd2 8. Qe5 $1 { Safeguarding the g3-pawn, while indirectly preparing the trade of the pawns on a2 and g6. } ( 8. Qxg6 $6 { (or 8 a4?) was less opportune due to } Qh2 { and }  Qxg3 ) 
Qxa2 9. Kg5 Qf2 ( { Or } 9... Qf7 10. Qf6 { - Taimanov. } ) 
10. Qf4 Qb6 11. Qf7+ Kc8 12. Qxg6 Qd8+ ( { On } 12... Qe3 { Taimanov had planned } 13. Kxh5 Qxf3 14. Qg4 $1 { and the same situation is reached two moves later. } ) 
13. Kxh5 Qd5+ 14. Kh4 Kb8 15. Qg5 Qd1 { The rest is easy, with the pawns shielding the king. } 16. Qf4+ Ka7 17. Qe3+ Ka6 18. g4 Qd6 19. f4 Qh6+ 20. Kg3 Kb5 21. Qd3+ Ka4 22. g5 
 1-0

