 { The centralization idea can also come handy in the presence of more pieces on the board. Here Black enjoys a healthy extra pawn, but before starting its advance he decides to limit the enemy queen. } 1... { -KEY- } Qd4 $1 { A perfect place for the queen, where she controls a lot of important squares and even eyes the f2-pawn. } ( { a) The obvious } 1... a5 { was less practical because of } 2. Qg7 $1 a4 3. Qb2 { with ideas like } Qb3 4. Qd4 a3 $2 5. Qd6 Kb5 6. Be2 Kb4 7. Qb6 { and White even wins. } ) 
 ( { b) While } 1... Ka5 { at once also complicates things, in view of } 2. Qc7 Kb4 3. Qb6 Kc3 4. Qa5 Qb4 5. Qa1 ) 
2. Qf8 { The following series of checks doesn't really lead White anywhere, but it was difficult to give him good advice. } Ka5 { King first! } ( { Of course } 2... a5 $2 3. Qb8 Ka6 4. Be2 { had to be avoided. } ) 
3. Qb8 Nd3 4. Qc7+ Kb4 5. Qb7+ Kc3 6. Qc7+ Kb2 $1 ( { Less is promised by } 6... Kd2 $6 7. Qa5 ) 
7. Qb7+ Kc1 8. Qc7+ Qc5 { Forcing an easily won minor piece ending. } 9. Qc6 Kd2 10. Qxc5 Nxc5 11. g4 fxg4 12. Bxg4 a5 13. f4 a4 14. f5 exf5 15. Bxf5 a3 16. Bb1 Kc1 17. Ba2 Kb2 ( { In view of } 18... Nb3 { coming next, White resigned. } ) 

 0-1

